# Panchayat Bill
Tamil title: ஊராட்சி மன்றச் சட்ட முன்வடிவு

The powers entrusted to them do not include powers such as instituting or hearing cases, nor do we insist that such powers must be granted. But if, when a person elected by the people becomes president, the administrative officer at his side is to perform every task, good people will grow increasingly reluctant to enter the panchayats and will withdraw.

Only those who submit to official interference, those who seek the favour of officials, and those who think it enough to enjoy the favour of the officer beside them even though they themselves possess no authority will come forward as presidents of bodies of local self-government. Those who would go there with the attitude, "I administer and run this village; I possess the ability required for it; I am responsible for the village's welfare; and I have the right to serve its people accordingly," will become increasingly disheartened, feeling that it is no proper place for them.

Therefore, if we are to produce rank upon rank of people well trained in democracy, the villages must be the first stage. The principle that democracy must begin in the villages, and that powers must be distributed to them on a large scale, has been emphasised not merely today or yesterday, but from the Royal Commission established in 1907 through the Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms, and thereafter in the Government of India Resolution of 1918 and the legislation enacted in 1918.

In these circumstances, we are obliged to consider the highly influential word "decentralisation" and the philosophy contained within it. Until now, we have viewed decentralisation only as the dispersal of concentrated power. Speaking for myself, whether this is regarded as going beyond the bill, as departing from the fundamental issue, or as introducing into this debate the separate policies which, as the Finance Minister says, each party possesses, I hold that if democracy is to come alive through decentralisation, distinct powers must be assigned to each region.

Just as this august House has assumed responsibility for the whole State, and just as our Ministers have assumed responsibility for their respective departments, bodies of local self-government must likewise be given full rights and full powers.

If members reflect upon Vinoba's *Rama Rajya*, which has been cited in this august House on many occasions, they will understand that this bill has been framed without even the slightest scent or trace of it. Even if the Select Committee accepted an arrangement under which administrative officers would run the panchayats; even if it is said that members pressed their views repeatedly in the Select Committee and ultimately arrived at a decision; and even if the matter was settled in the Select Committee, that does not mean it must necessarily be accepted here.

As the Leader of the Opposition, Ramasami Mudaliar, said, it can only mean that we stated and pressed our views and, when they were not accepted, arrived unanimously at a compromise. Even though the matter was discussed and decided in the Select Committee, we repeat those views in this august House out of the faint hope that the members of the Cabinet may soften a little, submit to the justice in them, show some interest in democracy, and accept the amendment.

We do not say this because we believe you will accept something merely because we have proposed it. We understand very well that you have neither acted in that manner nor will do so. But when we explain this measure to the village people, we must tell them the extent to which full powers have been granted to them.

In response, our Finance Minister said that they had been told they could themselves undertake and administer programmes such as the Community Project and National Extension, and that we ought to consider this substance rather than attend merely to words. Our Finance Minister, who practised for many years as a distinguished lawyer, now asks us in this Legislative Assembly not to attend to phrases and words. He will know that a single little phrase can save a person or send him to the gallows.

Therefore, instead of enticing us to disregard the wording and wait to see how the measure operates in practice, I humbly ask that it be written in clear and definite language, free of anything alarming, so that ordinary people like us can understand it.

"The Government may entrust to the panchayat union council, with its consent and on such terms as may be agreed upon, the execution in the panchayat development block of the National Extension Service Schemes."

It is an ordinary rule of interpretation that "may" implies "may not" as well. If the provision says "may entrust," one may ask; when may one ask? One may ask while the master is smiling. One need not ask when he is not smiling. If the request is made in the prescribed manner, it may be heard; otherwise, it may be rejected. The provision leaves room for all of this. I do not mean that you will necessarily say all these things.

If "may" were replaced by, "The Government shall entrust on representation by the panchayat union," we would have an opportunity to go among the village people and speak about your administration and its excellence and greatness. No one in this House can be so unaware of the difference between "may" and "shall." Our Finance Minister, who has successfully conducted murder trials, must have chosen that word only after examining the wording closely. I submit that changing "may" to "shall" would give the people considerable reassurance.

Another claim is that more money will be given to villages: however much house tax they collect, they will receive an equal matching grant. Like a loving mother who mixes honey with bitter medicine, asks her child to put out its tongue, and applies it, our Finance Minister offers this to us. I appreciate his maternal affection. But I suspect that the medicine you give contains much more bitterness and very little honey.

At present, the house tax collected amounts to twelve naye paise per head. You expect it to rise to thirty naye paise. You intend the tax to be increased to one and a half times its present level. The ruling party, with a numerical strength of more than 150 here, imposes taxes, whether justified or required by necessity, only while fearing that public resentment may turn against it. Do the Ministers expect any panchayat president to raise a house tax of one rupee to two and a half rupees?

**C. Subramaniam:** This does not provide for increasing the tax. Even the tax already imposed is not being collected. We have provided only that it should be collected. The tax exists now, but it is not collected.

**C. N. Annadurai:** The Taxation Enquiry Commission appointed by the Government recorded that villagers and village leaders were unwilling to collect taxes in panchayats: "The Taxation Enquiry Commission, however was of the view that when a panchayat is established, it should not start levying taxes immediately. Such a measure was bound to incur the resentment of the village people and if the panchayat were to refrain from such activity, they would earn contempt by inactivity."

Nothing can be accomplished without collecting money; collecting tax, however, earns a bad name. The Taxation Enquiry Commission thus noted in its report that there were impediments to collecting tax. The same condition continues in the villages today.

Instead of saying, "We will give you as much money as you collect in tax," the Government should show generosity in granting subsidies and loans, offering encouragement, and framing schemes whenever panchayats or panchayat unions come forward to undertake income-producing activities. If their leaders propose cultivating common land to earn revenue, or preparing mixed fertiliser and ask for the money needed as a loan, I urge the rulers to show generosity in financing all such ventures.
